{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 35, :comments ["<b>You shall do no unrighteousness in judgment.</b> He has already warned above, ״לא תעשו עול במשפט לא תשא פני דל״ — 'you shall do no unrighteousness in judgment; you shall not favor the poor.' But the judgment about which it speaks there concerns the laws of the Torah written in the section of Mishpatim, whereas that here concerns the laws of the lands and their statutes and regulations in matters of weights and measures, in the manner of what is written (Exodus 15:25): ״שם שם לו חק ומשפט״ — which, by way of the plain sense (peshat), refers to the laws and statutes of the wilderness." "<b>In measure.</b> This is the division of land; for example, brothers who divided land and measured for one of them in the rainy season — one does not measure for the second in the dry season with the same cord, because the cord is moist in the rainy season due to the abundance of moisture in the air, and it stretches and softens fully; but in the dry season it contracts and shortens, and the second brother suffers a loss." "<b>In weight.</b> That one should not bury one's weights in salt. And this is a warning to the seller not to deficient his measures and not to cause loss to the buyer, for since the salt draws out and removes the weights' tendency to stick, the weight becomes lighter. And thus they said in the Yerushalmi: because the salt draws out the weight." "<b>And in measure.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: that one should not make it foam. This משורה is a liquid measure, and when the seller fills the measure and makes it foam, the measure turns out to be deficient after a time. And this foaming is the scum that forms on the surface of the measure, called אשקומ׳א in the local tongue. Therefore one should let the measure rest a little until it settles fully and then go back and fill it properly. <b>And it may further include</b> under ובמשורה, which our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted as 'that one should not make it foam,' that one must also take care to pour the liquid gently so that it fills properly: for if one gives a measure of wine or oil hastily and quickly, the air enters into the measure and the liquid rises and the measure appears full at that moment, but the deficiency becomes noticeable after a time. <b>And our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted</b> that the משורה is a very small measure; and this is what they said: a fortiori — if regarding the משורה, which is one thirty-sixth of a log, the Torah was strict, then a fortiori for the hin and the half-hin and the third of a hin and the log and the half-log and the third of a log. And משורה was written only to incur transgression upon it from the time of foaming, even if one did not sell with it, just as they said regarding a weight, that one incurs transgression upon it from the time of making, even if one did not use it. <b>And thus we find</b> that King Solomon, peace be upon him, spoke of the evil traits of the heart and immediately juxtaposed and warned about measures of wood and stone; as he said (Proverbs 20:9): ״מי יאמר זכיתי לבי טהרתי מחטאתי״, and immediately juxtaposed to it: ״אבן ואבן איפה ואיפה תועבת ה' גם שניהם״ — to make known that just as a person is punished for an evil thought even if he did not carry it out in deed, so is he punished for making false and dishonest measures even if he did not actually use them to defraud anyone — he transgresses from the time of making. <b>Just as our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted</b> concerning idolatry: from the time of making, the curse applies, as is said (Deuteronomy 27:15): ״ארור האיש אשר יעשה פסל ומסכה״. And likewise again, from the time of making, the sin applies, as is said (Deuteronomy 9:21): ״ואת חטאתכם אשר עשיתם את העגל״. Therefore משורה was written, for surely if there is in this משורה the value of a perutah, it would already be derived from ״לא תגזול״; if there is not the value of a perutah, he does not transgress a negative commandment since the object itself is not money — and if so, why was it written? To make him liable from the time of foaming. <b>And our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted:</b> Said Rabbi Levi: the punishment for measures is harsher than the punishment for forbidden sexual relations, for forbidden sexual relations can be remedied through repentance as long as one has not produced offspring; but if one produced a mamzer, it is ״מעוות לא יוכל לתקן״ — 'that which is crooked cannot be made straight' — as our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted in Yevamot. But the punishment for measures, through which one robs the public, cannot be remedied through repentance, for one does not know to whom to restore it."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:27:27.939579369Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}